    "['mpls', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls interface all', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set protocols mpls interface all', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols mpls interface all\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if you want to be more specific about the interfaces (similar to the Huawei config where MPLS is enabled per interface), you could use:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls interface ge-1/0/1.0\\nset protocols mpls interface ge-1/0/3.0\\n##\\n\\nNote: The first translation enables MPLS on all interfaces, while the second specifically enables it on the two interfaces mentioned in the Huawei config (equivalent to GigabitEthernet1/0/1 and GigabitEthernet1/0/3). Juniper uses ge- instead of GigabitEthernet and adds .0 for the unit number.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls interface all'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls interface ge-1/0/1.0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set protocols mpls interface ge-1/0/1.0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols mpls interface all\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if you want to be more specific about the interfaces (similar to the Huawei config where MPLS is enabled per interface), you could use:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls interface ge-1/0/1.0\\nset protocols mpls interface ge-1/0/3.0\\n##\\n\\nNote: The first translation enables MPLS on all interfaces, while the second specifically enables it on the two interfaces mentioned in the Huawei config (equivalent to GigabitEthernet1/0/1 and GigabitEthernet1/0/3). Juniper uses ge- instead of GigabitEthernet and adds .0 for the unit number.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls interface ge-1/0/1.0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls interface ge-1/0/3.0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set protocols mpls interface ge-1/0/3.0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols mpls interface all\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if you want to be more specific about the interfaces (similar to the Huawei config where MPLS is enabled per interface), you could use:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls interface ge-1/0/1.0\\nset protocols mpls interface ge-1/0/3.0\\n##\\n\\nNote: The first translation enables MPLS on all interfaces, while the second specifically enables it on the two interfaces mentioned in the Huawei config (equivalent to GigabitEthernet1/0/1 and GigabitEthernet1/0/3). Juniper uses ge- instead of GigabitEthernet and adds .0 for the unit number.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls interface ge-1/0/3.0'}]]": 1,
    "['mpls ldp', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ldp interface all', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set protocols ldp interface all', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols ldp interface all\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Huawei `mpls ldp` command enables LDP (Label Distribution Protocol) globally. \\n- In Juniper, the equivalent is `set protocols ldp` to enable LDP globally.\\n- The `interface all` part is added since Huawei's configuration implicitly enables LDP on all interfaces where MPLS is enabled (as seen in the interface configurations), while Juniper requires explicit interface specification or `all` to cover all interfaces.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols ldp interface all'}]]": 1,
